<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the TaskTrooper.app bundle itself. electron-builder gives
  this file to `codesign` for the .app and gives entitlements.mac.inherit.plist
  to everything else inside it (the Electron helpers, and the Go backend under
  Contents/Resources/bin).

  The hardened runtime is on, because notarization requires it. Every key below
  is a hole in that runtime, so each one carries the reason it is here. If a
  reason stops being true, delete the key — this list only ever gets audited if
  it is short enough to read.

  NOT here, deliberately:
    - com.apple.security.app-sandbox — this app spawns and supervises arbitrary
      user tooling (the `claude` CLI, appium, adb, git, a Postgres cluster) and
      writes to a folder the user picks. The sandbox cannot express that, and a
      half-sandbox that needs a temporary exception for every one of those is
      worse than an honest unsandboxed Developer ID app.
    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory on the children —
      see the inherit plist; only the Electron helpers get it.
    - com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — nothing here scripts another
      application.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime. The hardened runtime
    forbids that by default, and without these two Electron launches to a blank
    window. `allow-jit` covers the MAP_JIT path; `allow-unsigned-executable-
    memory` covers the older W^X-violating path that V8 still uses on some code
    paths in Electron 40.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Library validation requires every Mach-O image loaded into this process to
    be signed by the same team as the main binary. Two things here need that
    relaxed:

      1. The ad-hoc fallback build (`-c.mac.identity=-`) carries no team id, so
         with library validation on the app refuses to load its own Electron
         Framework and dies before it draws a window. electron-builder documents
         this combination explicitly.
      2. Anything the app is ever asked to load out of a Homebrew or npm prefix
         is signed by whoever built it, not by us.

    It does NOT cover spawning a differently-signed child process — a child is
    validated as its own program, not as a library of ours — so this key is not
    what makes `claude` or the downloaded Postgres runnable. It is only about
    images loaded into this address space.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    macOS scrubs every DYLD_* variable out of the environment of a
    hardened-runtime process at launch, and the scrubbed environment is what
    children inherit. The supervisor spawns tooling it did not build and cannot
    relink — Homebrew's node (which appium runs on), adb, the user's `claude`
    CLI — and a Homebrew binary linked against a keg-only library needs its
    DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH to survive. This is the weakest-justified key in
    this file: if the supervisor is ever changed to hand each child an explicit,
    curated environment that contains no DYLD_* at all, remove it.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Outbound: the backend reaches Anthropic, GitHub and the repositories a task
    clones; the first start also downloads the Postgres binaries. Every leg
    between this app's own processes is loopback.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Inbound on loopback only. The backend, the embedder and Postgres each bind
    127.0.0.1 on a port they chose; Appium binds 127.0.0.1:4723. Nothing binds a
    routable interface and nothing is ever reachable from off this Mac — but
    binding a listening socket at all is what this key governs.

    Both network keys are App Sandbox keys and are inert in a Developer ID
    hardened-runtime build like this one. They are declared anyway so that the
    day someone tries a sandboxed or MAS build, it fails on something other
    than a socket the app has always opened.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
